WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT

MEASURED HERE, on one laptop, and reproducible by running the two commands in
the README:

  - every tensor shape in the loss path, and the ratio between the logits and
    the hidden states that produce them
  - the number of positions that contribute to a loss before and after padding
    and document-boundary masking
  - the loss of a freshly initialised model, and its perplexity against the
    vocabulary size
  - parameter counts for a tied and an untied output head at this width
  - peak resident set size for two ways of computing the same cross-entropy,
    repeated five times so the spread is visible
  - the loss curves of four short training runs: a two-head model, a correctly
    shifted single-head model, a deliberately mis-shifted one, and the same
    three repeated at 60, 150 and 300 steps

NOT MEASURED HERE:

  - anything on a GPU. Every figure is CPU, fp32, at laptop shapes.
  - anything about model quality. The training runs exist to show two specific
    effects and are far too short, on far too little text, to say whether any
    choice here trains a better model.
  - generalisation of any kind. The corpus is 35,941 tokens read 8.55 times
    over, so every loss quoted is a memorisation number.

ARITHMETIC, NOT MEASUREMENT: the parameter estimate uses the standard
12 x d_model^2 per block, which omits the embedding table and is approximate by
construction. Where a figure describes a configuration larger than the one run
here, it is arithmetic and is labelled as such on the page it appears on.

